 'On Linux, zero container CPU/memory limits inherit Pod limits only when PodLevelResources is enabled and Pod resources are set; explicit nonzero container limits win. CPU requests determine shares, memory limits set MemoryLimitInBytes, and enabled CFS quota converts CPU limits to quota/period; the exclusive-CPU feature can disable quota. Hugepage limits and conditional MemoryQoS/swap are constructed separately. Windows uses container CPU/memory limits to set CPU maximum and MemoryLimitInBytes, rather than Linux shares/quota; Pod-level resources are forbidden by the retained Windows Pod validator. Accepted requests reserve scheduling capacity but do not prevent OOM, throttling or eviction. The eviction manager evaluates observed pressure thresholds, tries node-level reclaim, then ranks/evicts Pods; observed Pod/container ephemeral-storage above their limits can also trigger immediate eviction. The node operator owns runtime support, actual usage and pressure recovery; inspect the Pod status and node pressure before retrying capacity-dependent startup.',
